      en: "Makhtesh Gadol is a large erosion crater in the Negev desert of Israel, measuring 14 km long and 6 km wide. Unlike meteorite craters, it formed through the erosion of softer rock layers, creating a steep-walled depression. The crater exposes a cross-section of geological strata, including Cretaceous limestone and sandstone. It is rich in marine fossils, such as ammonites. The site is a popular destination for geology enthusiasts and hikers. Its name means 'big mortar' in Hebrew.",
